{"kind":"artifact","text":"The remote a repository advertises is read from its own local configuration only, bounded, and admitted only when safe to link. Exact clean pushed head 512f5c5fc4367797839394b573df1e2944b7d50d on request/repo-remote-link; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 29ac34a8, checkout clean, main untouched at 02558e8d. Successor to d8c2e96d, under planner's dd883da0 and my promise 29b95948.\n\nTHE DEFECT CODEX FOUND, AND IT WAS THE ONE THAT MATTERED. gitRemotes ran git config without --local, so command-scope and global configuration contributed remote.origin.url. I reproduced it before changing anything: against a repository whose only local origin was the real one, an injected GIT_CONFIG_COUNT origin was emitted second, and gitRemotes keys a map by remote name, so the injected URL won. I had spent the whole first head building an allowlist for the URL's scheme and never asked whether the URL was the repository's to begin with. A scheme check on an attacker-supplied address is no protection at all.\n\nTHE FIX. config --local --null --get-regexp. Three regressions follow an injected outer-scope origin through the layers it would have to cross: TestGitRemotesReadsRepositoryLocalConfigurationOnly, which also proves an outer scope cannot introduce a remote rather than only overwrite one; TestLocalWorktreesIgnoresOutOfRepositoryRemoteConfiguration; and TestWorktreesResponseIgnoresOutOfRepositoryRemoteConfiguration against the live response body.\n\nBOUNDS, FAILING CLOSED. 64 KiB of configuration output and 64 remotes. Both refuse rather than truncate, because a truncated set would make which remote gets linked depend on where reading stopped — a quieter failure than no link at all.\n\nQUERY AND FRAGMENT ARE NOW REJECTED, and codex was right that this was the real inconsistency rather than a gap. I had declined userinfo because it may carry a credential, then passed ?access_token=SECRET through to the API, the DOM and the href. One rule applied to one syntax. I rejected rather than narrowing the policy because what is being linked is a repository's own address, which needs neither component, so refusing costs nothing real — and the alternative, judging which parameter names are secret, is an enumerated denylist, precisely the shape the scheme rule is careful not to be. Both layers test the serialisation rather than parsed fields: url.Parse escapes ? and # wherever they appear in a host or path, so a bare one in the output can only be the delimiter, the empty one ForceQuery records included.\n\nA GAP IN MY OWN EARLIER TESTS. The pre-existing org-alias:org/repo.git case did not force an allowlist — it carries no host, so the host check refuses it under a denylist too. future+git:// and x-unheard-of:// parse cleanly with a host, so only the scheme rule can refuse them. Both are now pinned at the Go unit, service-endpoint, TypeScript unit and rendered-DOM layers.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN ON REPORT. Removing --local fails TestGitRemotesReadsRepositoryLocalConfigurationOnly and TestLocalWorktreesIgnoresOutOfRepositoryRemoteConfiguration, both reporting the attacker URL where the repository's own origin belongs. Restored; internal/app/app.go sha256 is 1088a87d577f49a737b3aa8aa322ef9f584d386ea55b8e8f873edcb7cd248e21 before and after, byte-identical.\n\nONE MEASUREMENT I HAVE TO BE HONEST ABOUT. My first full suite at this head failed: cmd/gs hit the ten-minute package timeout. That was my own doing — a second agent's suite was running and I mutated app.go in this worktree while a background suite was reading it. I did not call it flaky. cmd/gs then passed alone at 403.972s, and a clean full run afterwards passed at 461.363s. The green below is that clean run.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from the clean run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached; npm test --prefix ui 98 tests, 98 pass, 0 fail; gofmt -l . empty; go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"512f5c5fc4367797839394b573df1e2944b7d50d","path":"internal/app"}}